.docname {Rooms}
.include {docs}

How vCortex finds the rooms of a building. A room is found **twice**, on purpose:

| When | Name | What it gives | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Before** the walls | the free floor | Where the robot could stand, cut into rooms at doors | The wall step needs to know where to start walking from |
| **After** the walls | the final rooms | Each room closed by its walls: outline, walls around it, closed or open | This is the room the twin holds |

The walls need a room to start from, and the room needs walls to be closed. The free floor breaks
that circle: it is a rough room, made only from the robot's path and the low dots.

## In short

1. **Look from above.** Lay a grid of 5 cm squares over the map. For each square, note whether the
   floor was seen, whether something low stands there (a leg, a wall), and whether something tall
   stands there (a wall, a wardrobe).
2. **Free floor.** Start where the robot stood and spread over the floor it could walk on, like
   water poured on the floor. This is the rough room.
3. **Cut at doors.** Spread again, but with a 1 m wide coin that must fit between the tall things.
   Most doors are narrower than the coin, so it cannot pass: the two sides become two rooms.
4. **Walls next.** The [Walls](walls.qd) step walks out from these rough rooms and finds the walls.
5. **Final rooms.** Draw the walls on the grid, close the doors, and pour the water again. Where it
   stops is the room. If it runs off the edge of the map, a wall is missing: the room is **open**.

**Example, real:** the room of the [Overview](introduction.qd), 260 robot positions. The free floor
is 24 m², less than half of the 53 m² room, because furniture hides the rest. That is enough: the
wall step only needs a place inside the room to start from.

The rest of this page gives each step in detail, with its numbers.

.mermaid caption:{Where the two room steps sit in one model run.}
    flowchart LR
        A[Clean, floor,<br/>square the map] --> B[Free floor<br/>rough rooms]
        B --> C[Walls<br/>walk out of each room]
        C --> D[Objects]
        C --> E[Final rooms<br/>closed by the walls]

## Words used on this page

| Word | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **Grid** | A top view of the map cut into squares. One square, a **cell**, is 5 cm |
| **Squared map** | The map turned so the walls run along the x and y axes (see the `yaw` in the report) |
| **Floor plane** | The tilted plane the floor lies on. A **height** is always measured above it |
| **Pose** | One place the robot stood. 260 of them in the real run below |
| **Flood** (flood fill) | Like water poured on one cell: it spreads to the 4 cells next to it (up, down, left, right), again and again, until something stops it. Every cell it reaches gets the same number. The usual name of this method is **flood fill** |

Cells are joined by their 4 sides, never by corners. Two areas that touch only at a corner stay
apart.

## Step A: the free floor

**In:** every dot (squared), the floor plane, the grid, the poses. **Out:** a room number for each
cell (0 = no room), how many rooms, their area. Code: `model/floor/free_space.cpp`.

### A1. Sort the cells

**Code:** [`free_space.cpp:25`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L25) `classify_cells` · [`free_space.cpp:37`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L37) `low band` · [`free_space.cpp:41`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L41) `floor_hit` · [`free_space.cpp:42`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L42) `tall` · [`free_space.cpp:46`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L46) `blocked`

Each cell gets three flags, from the heights of the dots inside it.

| Flag | True when | Means |
|---|---|---|
| `floor_hit` | A dot lies within 8 cm of the floor plane (`floor_band`) | The floor was seen here |
| `blocked` | At least 2 dots (`min_points`) lie 10 to 30 cm above the floor (`low_lo`, `low_hi`) | Something stands here: a wall, a leg, a box |
| `tall` | A dot lies at 90 cm or more above the floor (`tall_from`) | A wall or a wardrobe. Desks stay below this height |

**Example, five cells.** Heights are in metres above the floor. (Made-up numbers.)

| Cell | Dots at | `floor_hit` | `blocked` | `tall` | What it is |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 0.01, 0.03, 0.02 | yes | no | no | Open floor |
| 2 | 0.02, 0.15, 0.22, 0.28 | yes | yes (3 low dots) | no | A desk leg |
| 3 | 0.03, 0.12, 0.25, 1.00, 1.80 | yes | yes | yes | A wall |
| 4 | 0.04, 0.75 | yes | no | no | Floor **under a desk**: the top (0.75 m) is in no layer |
| 5 | none | no | no | no | Never seen |

Cell 4 is why the layers are chosen like this: a desk does not block the floor under it, only its
legs do.

![Five cells sorted by the height of their dots](images/rooms-cell-classes.svg "One column of dots per cell, with the three layers behind them.")

### A2. Fill one-cell holes in the seen floor

**Code:** [`grids.cpp:63`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/geometry/grids.cpp#L63) `close_holes` · [`free_space.cpp:137`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L137) `call`

The lidar misses a cell here and there. The step grows the `floor_hit` cells by one cell, then
shrinks them by one cell. A hole of one cell is filled; the outside edge stays where it was. The
result is called `seen`.

### A3. The floor the robot could reach

**Code:** [`free_space.cpp:93`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L93) `label_area_reached` · [`free_space.cpp:67`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L67) `spread_labels` · [`free_space.cpp:53`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L53) `disk_fits` · [`free_space.cpp:140`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L140) `call`

Flood from each pose over cells that are `seen` and **not** `blocked`.

- A pose starts a flood only if its own cell is `seen`, not blocked, and not reached yet.
- Each flood gets the next number: 1, 2, …
- Cells the robot could not stand on, or never saw, are left out.

The result is `floor_areas`.

### A4. The room cores: cut at doors

**Code:** [`free_space.cpp:138`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L138) `door disk` · [`free_space.cpp:141`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L141) `call`

Flood again from each pose, but now with a **disk** (a round stamp) of radius `door_radius` =
0.5 m = 10 cells. A cell is allowed only when the whole disk, centred on it, covers no `tall`
cell.

- The disk is 317 cells; it needs a clear space **1.0 m wide**.
- A door narrower than 1.0 m cannot hold the disk: the flood cannot pass, so the two sides get
  **two numbers**.
- A desk is not `tall`, so an aisle between desks never cuts a room.

![A stamp of 1.0 m in a door, an aisle and a closet](images/rooms-door-disk.svg "The stamp needs 1.0 m of floor with no tall cell.")

**Examples from the tests** (real drawings in `backend/tests/model/floor/free_space_test.cpp`;
one character is 10 cm):

| Test | Drawing | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Two rooms of 1.6 × 1.8 m, door of **0.9 m** | wall with a gap, a pose in each room | **2 rooms**: 0.9 < 1.0 |
| A room of 3.6 × 2.0 m, two desks leave an aisle of **0.8 m** | desks are not `tall` | **1 room** |
| A closet of 1.2 × 1.2 m behind a 0.8 m door | 1.2 m is wide enough for the disk | **2 rooms** |
| The same two rooms, but the robot never entered the right one | no pose there | **1 room**: it opens only from a pose |

The last row matters: a room seen through a door but never walked belongs to the room next to it,
until the robot walks in.

### A5. Grow the rooms

**Code:** [`free_space.cpp:116`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L116) `expand_rooms_labels` · [`free_space.cpp:143`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L143) `call`

The cores are smaller than the rooms: the disk keeps them away from walls and door frames. So all
cores grow together, one cell at a time, over `floor_areas` that are not `blocked`. Two rooms meet
**halfway through the door** between them.

![Floor reached, room cores and grown rooms on the two-room test drawing](images/rooms-free-floor-steps.svg "Steps A3 to A5, run on the test drawing.")

### A6. Area

**Code:** [`free_space.cpp:148`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L148) `area` · [`free_space.cpp:129`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/floor/free_space.cpp#L129) `find_floor_rooms`

Area = (cells with a room number) × 0.05 × 0.05.

.mermaid caption:{Step A in one picture.}
    flowchart TD
        P[Poses] --> F
        D[Dots] --> S[A1 sort cells:<br/>floor_hit, blocked, tall]
        S --> H[A2 fill one-cell holes<br/>seen]
        H --> F[A3 flood from poses over<br/>seen and not blocked<br/>floor_areas]
        F --> C[A4 flood with a 1.0 m disk<br/>avoiding tall cells<br/>room cores]
        C --> G[A5 grow cores<br/>over floor_areas]
        G --> O[Room per cell, area]

### Real numbers

The room of the [Overview](introduction.qd), 260 poses:

| | Value |
|---|---|
| Rooms found | 1 |
| Free floor | 24.03 m² (9,610 cells) |
| The room, walls to walls | about 8.4 × 6.4 m = 53 m² |

The free floor is **less than half** of the room: furniture is left out, and so is floor the lidar
never saw. That is enough for what comes next: the wall step only needs a place **inside** the room
to walk out from.

## Step B: the final rooms

**In:** the walls, the grid, the poses, `door_max`, the room settings. **Out:** the list of rooms.
Code: `model/rooms.cpp`. It runs after the [walls](walls.qd) are found.

### B1. Draw the walls on the grid

**Code:** [`rooms.cpp:26`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L26) `body_of` · [`rooms.cpp:51`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L51) `Mask::block` · [`rooms.cpp:151`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L151) `call`

Each wall is a box: its centre line, plus half its thickness on each side. Every cell the box
**touches** is set to blocked, not only the cells whose centre it covers. So a thin wall cannot
slip between two cells and leave a hole.

### B2. Close the doors

**Code:** [`rooms.cpp:77`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L77) `close_doors` · [`rooms.cpp:81`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L81) `gap rule`

Two wall pieces on one line, with a gap between them, can be the two sides of a door. If the gap
is **longer than 0 and at most `door_max` = 1.2 m**, a closing box is drawn in the gap, as thick as
the thinner wall.

| Gap between two pieces of one wall | Result |
|---|---|
| 0.9 m | Closed: it is a door, and the room stays shut |
| 1.2 m | Closed (the limit counts) |
| 1.5 m | Left open: the flood goes through |

### B3. Flood from each pose

**Code:** [`rooms.cpp:104`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L104) `flood` · [`rooms.cpp:116`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L116) `leak` · [`rooms.cpp:166`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L166) `min_area`

For each pose whose cell is free and not labelled yet:

1. Flood from it, over cells that are not blocked, 4 sides only (a diagonal step would slip
   between two wall cells that meet at a corner).
2. Count the cells. Under `min_area` = 1.0 m² (400 cells) it is a pocket, not a room: skip it.
3. If the flood **reached the edge of the grid**, a wall is missing: the room is **open**
   (`closed` is false). Otherwise it is closed.

Cells never seen are not blocked, so a flood that gets through a gap runs on over unseen space to
the edge. That is how a missing wall shows up.

![A closed room and an open room after the flood](images/rooms-final-flood.svg "Same room, two gaps: 0.9 m is closed, 1.5 m leaks.")

### B4. The room

**Code:** [`rooms.cpp:174`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L174) `outline box` · [`rooms.cpp:130`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L130) `touches` · [`rooms.cpp:185`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/rooms.cpp#L185) `ids by corner`

- **Outline:** the **box** around the cells the flood reached. It is turned back to the map's own
  angle later, with everything else.
- **Walls around it:** a wall belongs to the room when its inner face is within
  `wall_touch_distance` = 15 cm of one side of the box and runs along that side.
- **Id:** the rooms are sorted by their lower-left corner (x first, then y), then numbered
  `room-1`, `room-2`, … So the same map gives the same ids, however the robot walked.

### B5. Objects join a room

**Code:** [`model.cpp:93`](https://github.com/unibo-dslab-projects/vCortex/blob/main/backend/src/model/model.cpp#L93) `room_id`

Back in the model, an object gets a `room_id` when its centre lies inside the outline of a
**closed** room. The first closed room that holds it wins.

### Real numbers

| | Value |
|---|---|
| Poses | 260 |
| Rooms | 1 (`room-1`), closed |
| Outline, squared map | x from −6.60 to 1.75, y from −3.35 to 3.05: **8.35 × 6.40 m** |
| Walls around it | all 4: `wall-1` to `wall-4` |
| Objects in it | 19 of 19 |

## The settings of this chapter

| Setting | Default | Used in |
|---|---|---|
| `free_space.floor_band` | 0.08 m | A1 |
| `free_space.low_lo`, `low_hi` | 0.10 m, 0.30 m | A1 |
| `free_space.min_points` | 2 | A1 |
| `free_space.tall_from` | 0.90 m | A1 |
| `free_space.door_radius` | 0.50 m | A4 |
| `walls.door_max` | 1.2 m | B2 |
| `rooms.min_area` | 1.0 m² | B3 |
| `rooms.wall_touch_distance` | 0.15 m | B4 |

All of them are explained in [Settings](settings.qd).

